---
title: Packaging and sharing
description: Create a complete Windows app folder and understand the preview's distribution limits.
sidebar:
  order: 5
---

From your project directory, use the matched SDK CLI:

```powershell
lui check
lui build --output Dist/MyApp
```

If `lui` is not on PATH, use the full path to the extracted SDK's `lui.cmd`. Choose an output directory that does not already exist.

Share the entire output folder. Recipients launch `Start-LUI.cmd` and do not need the developer SDK. Sending only the executable leaves out required runtime and application files.

The folder includes prepared application content. New modules, dependencies and assets must be declared before packaging. Keep dependency notices and license material with redistributions.

## Try the result independently

Run the packaged copy, not the original source project. Check startup, navigation, input, shutdown and error behavior. Try it on a separate target machine before treating it as ready to distribute.

This preview produces a folder, not a signed installer, automatic updater or stable distribution channel. Clean-machine prerequisites, signing/publisher authenticity and final release/redistribution checks remain open. Existing-worker smoke checks do not qualify every Windows PC.

## Keep versions deliberate

The SDK and project pin identify matched components. Do not swap native libraries or checker binaries from another SDK to repair a package. Install the required SDK beside other versions and build from a complete matching installation.
